    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d313">

                <pb facs="0688" n="649" xml:id="pg649"/>

                <head>313. Letter From President Reagan to Tunisian President Bourguiba<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d313-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1984-01-09">January 9, 1984</date>
                    </dateline>
                    <salute>Dear Mr. President:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>I have sent Dick Walters, a trusted aide, to speak with you about my concern over
                    the recent unrest in Tunisia. I applaud your courage in facing up to Tunisia’s
                    economic challenge and I understand how difficult it was to deal with the
                    disorders. Tunisia’s friends will now want to consider how they can help your
                    government and we in the United States are considering ways to do so.</p>
                <p>With regard to the external threat, I have requested General Walters to share
                    with you our latest assessment of Libyan activities in the region, and to
                    reassure you of our continuing efforts to monitor Libyan activities. The United
                    States remains concerned for the continued security and territorial integrity of
                    Tunisia.</p>
                <p>I know you will speak candidly to Dick Walters and I look forward to a report
                    from him upon his return.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d313-f2"/></p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Ronald Reagan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
